tel 1.0

base guillotine
import soundness

info   external processes as typed values: commands checked at compile time, run, piped and read as types
detail
    _Guillotine_ runs external programs. A command is a `Command` — a list of arguments, with no
    shell in between — built by the `sh"…"` interpolator, which parses quotes and escapes as a
    POSIX shell would **while the code compiles**: an unclosed quote is a compile error, and a
    value substituted into the command is escaped for the position it lands in, so a filename
    with a space stays one argument. Commands join into a `Pipeline` with `|`, and either runs
    synchronously with `exec[T]()`, whose type parameter decides how the process's output is
    read, or starts in the background with `fork[T]()`, which returns a `Job` to write to, read
    from, await, abort or kill.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    val file = t"notes of the day.txt"
    val lines: List[Text] = sh"cat $file".exec[List[Text]]()
    val status: Exit = sh"false".exec[Exit]()
    val count: Text = (sh"cat $file" | sh"wc -l").exec[Text]().trim
    ```

    Every run needs four contextual values: a `WorkingDirectory` (the process's working
    directory; `workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory` is this JVM's), an `Environment`
    (its variables; `environments.javaBaseEnvironment` inherits this process's,
    `environments.emptyEnvironment` gives it none, and `variables(…)` adds to either), a
    `Tactic[Exec.Error]` (`strategies.throwUnsafely`, or a `tend`/`mitigate` block), and an
    `Exec.Event is Loggable` (`logging.silentLogging`, or a real log). None of these comes with
    `import soundness.*`, since each selects a policy: `import pkg.*` does not import givens,
    so each is imported by name.

    The result type of `exec` and `fork` is anything with a `Computable` instance: `Text` and
    `String` (the whole of standard output, read when the process ends), `List[Text]` and
    `Chain[Text]` (its lines, the chain pulled lazily as the process writes), `Data` (the raw
    bytes), `Stderr` (standard error as text), `Exit` (the exit status, `Exit.Ok` or
    `Exit.Fail(code)`), `Unit` (wait, and discard the output), and any path type (the trimmed
    output, decoded). A command whose name is a POSIX utility may be applied with no type at
    all — `sh"echo hi"()` — and the `Intelligible` givens in `PosixCommands` choose the result
    type the command implies.

    A command may also be run on a pseudo-terminal with `Command#pty`, so that it finds a
    terminal on its standard streams, and driven interactively through a `session`; its line
    discipline is a `Termios`. A process which should outlive this one is started with
    `detach`, which keeps only its `Pid`. `Process` is a handle to any process on the system,
    by `Pid`, with its parent, children, start time and CPU time; `Pid.Error` is raised when no
    such process is running.

    The library is the single component `guillotine.core`, which compiles for the JVM and
    Scala Native (not Scala.js, which has no `ProcessBuilder`). Three private pieces are split
    by platform: the pseudo-terminal (through the foreign function API on the JVM, through C
    interop on Native, on Linux and macOS in both cases), the wiring of a pipeline (the JDK's
    `startPipeline` on the JVM; on Native each stage is started on pipes and a daemon thread
    copies its predecessor's output into it), and a process's start time and CPU time, which
    the Native javalib cannot report, so `Process#startTime` and `Process#cpuUsage` are `Unset`
    there. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer guillotine.Command
refer guillotine.sh
refer guillotine.Job
refer guillotine.Computable
refer guillotine.Process
refer guillotine.Exec

entry .sh   the `sh"…"` interpolator: a `Command` parsed as the code compiles, with escaped substitutions
    `sh"ls -la $directory"` is a `Command` whose arguments are the words of the literal, split on
    whitespace with single quotes, double quotes and backslash escapes honoured as a POSIX shell
    honours them. No shell runs: the arguments go straight to the program, so a metacharacter
    in a substituted value is never interpreted. The parsing happens while the code compiles,
    through the `Sh.Runtime` parser, and an error is reported at the character which caused
    it: _this single quote is never closed_ and _this double quote is never closed_ at the
    quote that was opened, _a command cannot end with an escape character_ at the trailing
    backslash, and _an escape character cannot appear immediately before a substitution_ at a
    backslash written before a `$`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val file = t"a file.txt"
    sh"cat $file"                   // Command(t"cat", t"a file.txt"): one argument
    sh"""echo "quoted $file" x"""   // Command(t"echo", t"quoted a file.txt", t"x")
    sh"echo 'Hello world"           // does not compile
    ```

    A substituted value is escaped for the position it lands in. Unquoted, a `Text` is exactly
    one argument whatever it contains, and a `List[Text]` is one argument per element (an empty
    list contributes none); inside quotes, a list's elements are joined with spaces into the
    one argument the quotes delimit. A value adjacent to literal text joins it (`${a}x` gives
    `ax`). A `Command` substitutes as its arguments, so that `sh"sh -c '$inner'"` passes an
    inner command to a shell as a single argument. Any other type substitutes through a
    `Parameterizable` instance — `Pid` renders as `↯42`, and anything `Encodable in Text`
    (a `Path`, a URL) as its encoding — and a type with none is a compile error, _can't
    substitute `T` into a sh-interpolator_. The selection of a `val` member of an enclosing
    class (`$config.host`) cannot be substituted; bind it to a local first.

    The result's static type is a refinement, `Command { type Exec = "ls" }`, carrying the
    command's name as a literal type, which is what `Executable#apply()` uses to find the
    `Intelligible` evidence giving the command's implied result type. Two commands written
    with different whitespace or quoting are equal when their arguments are.

entry .Executable   what can be run: a `Command` or a `Pipeline`, with `exec`, `fork`, `detach` and `|`
  detail
      The sealed trait of the two runnable things, `Command` and `Pipeline`, and the home of
      the operations shared by both. Each run takes a `WorkingDirectory` and an `Environment`
      for the child (an environment which can enumerate its variables replaces this process's
      entirely, and a bare command name is then looked up on *that* environment's `PATH`,
      since the JDK would search this JVM's), a `Tactic[Exec.Error]` for a process which
      cannot be started, and an `Exec.Event is Loggable` for the start, exit, abort and kill
      events.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      sh"echo hi".exec[Text]()                    // waits; t"hi\n"
      val job = sh"sleep 5".fork[Unit]()          // returns at once
      job.abort()
      (sh"echo a" | sh"tr a b").exec[Text]()      // a pipeline runs the same way
      ```

      `exec` waits for the process and interprets its output as the type named; `fork` starts
      it and returns the `Job`; `detach` starts it and keeps only its `Pid`. The nullary
      `apply()` is `exec` with the result type implied by the command's name.
  refer guillotine.Command
  refer guillotine.Pipeline
  refer guillotine.Job
  refer guillotine.Computable
  entry #Exec   the command's name as a literal type, when known
      A `Command` built by `sh"…"` has `Exec` set to its first word (`sh"ls -la"` is a `Command
      { type Exec = "ls" }`); one built with `Command(…)` leaves it abstract. `apply()` uses it
      to summon `Exec is Intelligible`, the evidence for the result type a command implies, and
      a `Job`'s first type parameter is it.
  entry #fork   starts the process and returns its `Job` without waiting
      `fork[result]()` starts the command (or every stage of a pipeline, wired together) and
      returns a `Job[Exec, result]^` at once: the result type is fixed now, and read with
      `Job#await()` later. The job is a capability, tracked fresh, so it cannot escape into an
      untracked value. Needs a `WorkingDirectory`, an `Environment`, a `Tactic[Exec.Error]`
      (raised when the program cannot be started) and an `Exec.Event is Loggable`, and logs
      `Exec.Event.ProcessStart` or `PipelineStart` at `info`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val job = sh"cat".fork[Text]()
      job.stdin(Stream(Data(104, 105, 10)))
      job.await().trim   // t"hi"
      ```
  entry #detach   starts the process to outlive this one, keeping only its `Pid`
      For a child which should run beyond this program — a server, a daemon. Its standard
      input is the null device, and its output and errors are discarded (`detach()`) or
      appended to a log file (`detach(log)`, taking any path type `Abstractable across Paths
      to Text`, and making the log's parent directories). A `fork`ed child inherits pipes
      nobody drains, and a server writing to them eventually blocks; a detached one cannot.
      For a pipeline, the head reads the null device, the tail writes the log, and every
      stage's errors go with the tail's; the `Pid` is the tail's. The child's environment is
      the `Environment` in scope, so `environments.emptyEnvironment` with `variables(…)` gives
      it exactly the variables named. Logs `ProcessStart`, and raises `Exec.Error` if the
      program cannot be started.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val pid: Pid = sh"server --port 8080".detach(t"/var/log/server.log")
      ```
  entry #exec   runs the process to completion and reads its output as the type named
      `exec[result]()` is `fork[result]().await()`: the process runs, and its output is
      interpreted by the `result is Computable` instance in scope — `Text`, `List[Text]`,
      `Chain[Text]`, `Data`, `Stderr`, `Exit`, `Unit`, or a path type (see `Computable`).
      The type decides more than a conversion: which stream is read, whether a non-zero status
      is a failure or an answer, and whether the call returns when the process ends (`Text`)
      or as soon as it has started (`Chain[Text]`, whose lines arrive lazily).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      sh"echo hello".exec[Text]().trim            // t"hello"
      sh"printf 'a\nb\nc\n'".exec[List[Text]]()   // List(t"a", t"b", t"c")
      sh"false".exec[Exit]()                      // Exit.Fail(1)
      sh"sh -c 'echo oops 1>&2'".exec[Stderr]()   // Stderr(t"oops\n")
      ```

      `Exec.Error` is raised when the program cannot be started — a missing binary, a
      directory which is not executable — and names the command that failed; a read of the
      output which is cut short (a `Truncation.Error`) is reported as the same error, with the
      last command of a pipeline as its command. A program which runs and exits non-zero is
      not an error: ask for an `Exit` to see its status.
  entry #apply   `command()`: runs it with the result type its name implies; `a(b)`: the pipeline `b | a`
      The nullary `apply()` runs the command as `exec` does, with the result type chosen by
      the `Intelligible` evidence for the command's `Exec` name: `sh"echo hi"()` is a `Text`,
      `sh"ls"()` a `Chain[Text]`, `sh"sleep 1"()` an `Exit`, as the givens in `PosixCommands`
      say. It is `inline`, and needs the same `WorkingDirectory`, `Environment`, `Computable`,
      `Tactic[Exec.Error]` and `Exec.Event is Loggable` as `exec`. A command whose name has no
      `Intelligible` instance, or which was built with `Command(…)` rather than `sh"…"`, does
      not compile this way: name the type with `exec[T]()`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      sh"echo hi"().trim                   // t"hi"
      sh"head -n 1 /dev/null"().to[List]   // Nil
      sh"sleep 0.01"()                     // Exit.Ok
      ```

      `apply(command: Executable)` is the other way round from `|`: `b(a)` is `a | b`, the
      pipeline in which `a`'s output feeds `b`. Both flatten, so a pipeline applied to a
      pipeline is one pipeline of all their commands.
  entry #|   `a | b`: the pipeline whose first stage's output is its second's input
      Builds a `Pipeline`, flattening: `a | b | c` is one `Pipeline` of three commands, and a
      pipeline piped into a command, or a command into a pipeline, extends it rather than
      nesting. The pipeline's output and exit status are its last command's, and input written
      to it goes to its first.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      (sh"echo a" | sh"cat" | sh"wc").commands.length   // 3
      ```

entry .Command   a program and its arguments, as a value: built by `sh"…"`, run with `exec`, `fork` or `pty`
  detail
      A `Command` is a list of `#arguments`, the first naming the program, with no shell in
      between: each argument reaches the program exactly as it is held. It is an
      `Executable`, so `exec[T]()`, `fork[T]()`, `detach`, `apply()` and `|` are the ways to
      run and combine it. `sh"…"` is the usual way to build one, and `Command(t"ls", t"-la")`
      the direct one; the two are equal when their arguments are, though only the interpolated
      form carries its name in its `Exec` type.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A command, with a value escaped into one argument
        code
            import soundness.*

            val file = t"notes of the day.txt"
            sh"cat $file"                         // Command(t"cat", t"notes of the day.txt")
      frame
        label Piped into a second command
        code
            import soundness.*

            val file = t"notes of the day.txt"
            sh"cat $file" | sh"grep -c TODO"      // a Pipeline of two commands
      frame
        label Run, with its output read as the type named
        code
            import soundness.*
            import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

            val file = t"notes of the day.txt"
            val todos: Int = (sh"cat $file" | sh"grep -c TODO").exec[Text]().trim.decode[Int]
      ##
      ```

      A command renders two ways: `show` gives it as it would be typed at a shell, each argument
      quoted only if it needs to be (`echo 'a b'`), and `inspect` gives the source which would
      build it (`sh"echo 'a b'"`, in triple quotes if a double quote is needed). `#escape`
      gives the strict form, every argument single-quoted, for passing a whole command to a
      shell. A command is also substitutable into another `sh"…"`, as its arguments.

      On the JVM and on Scala Native, `#pty` runs the command on a pseudo-terminal rather than
      on pipes; `Command.OnTerminal` is the value it returns.
  refer guillotine.sh
  refer guillotine.Executable
  refer guillotine.Pipeline
  refer guillotine.Command.OnTerminal
  entry .inspectable   renders a command as the `sh"…"` source which builds it
      `sh"echo 'a b'"`, with each argument quoted as `show` quotes it, and triple-quoted when an
      argument contains a double quote. The given is bounded over subtypes of `Command`, so
      the refined type `sh"…"` expands to finds it too.
  entry .showable   renders a command as it would be typed at a shell
      Each argument is written bare if it is plain, in single quotes if it contains a space,
      tab, backslash or double quote, in double quotes if it contains a single quote, and in
      double quotes with the double quotes escaped if it contains both; the arguments are
      joined with spaces. `sh"echo 'a b'".show` is `echo 'a b'`.
  entry .OnTerminal   a command to be run on a fresh pseudo-terminal of a given size
    detail
        What `Command#pty` returns: the command, the terminal's `#width` and `#height` in
        characters, and the `#termios` line discipline to set before it starts, or `Unset` to
        leave the system's. It is a description, not a terminal: each `#exec` or `session`
        allocates a pseudo-terminal with `posix_openpt`, runs the command on its slave side as
        a session leader (so the terminal is its controlling terminal, and `^C` typed at it
        interrupts it), and releases the terminal afterwards. On the JVM this goes through the
        foreign function API, so run with `--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` to silence the
        JDK's warning; on Scala Native through C interop. Linux and macOS only.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

        sh"stty size".pty(100, 30).exec[Text]().trim   // t"30 100"

        sh"sh -c 'read line; stty size'".pty().session: terminal ?=>
          terminal.resize(132, 50)   // the program receives SIGWINCH
          t"\r".writeTo(terminal)    // press Return
          terminal.text().trim       // t"50 132"
        ```

        A `session` (aperture's extension, through `.sessional`) lends the running command to
        a block as a `Command.OnTerminal.Job`, and ends it when the block returns or throws:
        a command still running is hung up on (`SIGHUP`), as closing a real terminal would,
        and killed if it has not exited within a second. The job's output is everything the
        program writes to the terminal, standard error included, escape sequences and all;
        anything `Writable` to it — `Text`, `Data`, a stream — is typed at the terminal. A
        terminal has no end of input: a program reading until end of file is sent the
        end-of-file character, `^D` (byte 4), rather than having its input closed.
    refer guillotine.Termios
    refer guillotine.Command.OnTerminal.Job
    entry .showable   renders the command and its terminal's size: `stty size on 80×24`
    entry .Sessional   the `Sessional` instance class: lends a running `OnTerminal.Job` to a block
        The class behind `.sessional`, a named class (as a `Sessional` instance must be) which
        takes the `WorkingDirectory`, `Environment`, `Tactic[Exec.Error]` and `Exec.Event is
        Loggable` the command's run needs, and whose `session` starts the command on a fresh
        terminal, passes its `Job` to the block as a contextual value, and ends it afterwards.
    entry .sessional   the given which makes `onTerminal.session: terminal ?=> …` work
        In implicit scope, so it needs no import, but it needs a `WorkingDirectory`, an
        `Environment`, a `Tactic[Exec.Error]` and an `Exec.Event is Loggable` in scope where
        `session` is called. The block receives a `Command.OnTerminal.Job` as its contextual
        `session` value, which may be used inside the block but not kept.
    entry .Job   a `Job` running on a pseudo-terminal: resizable, with a settable line discipline
      detail
          The handle a `session` lends: a `guillotine.Job` whose result type is `Exit`, with
          everything a job has — `text()`, `stdin`, `await()`, `exitStatus()`, `kill()` — and
          three things only a terminal has. Its output stream is the terminal's, carrying
          standard error too, and its error stream is empty.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          import soundness.*
          import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
          import logging.silentLogging
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

          sh"head -c 2".pty(termios = Termios.raw).session: terminal ?=>
            t"hi".writeTo(terminal)      // delivered at once, without a newline
            terminal.mode.canonical      // false
            terminal.mode = Termios.cooked
            terminal.text()              // t"hi", unechoed
          ```
      entry #resize   sets the terminal's size, in characters; the program receives `SIGWINCH`
      entry #mode   the terminal's line discipline, as a `Termios`, readable and assignable while the command runs
          `terminal.mode` reads the switches `Termios` names from the terminal, and
          `terminal.mode = Termios.raw` sets them, leaving the rest of the discipline as it
          is — what the program itself would do with `stty`. Turning `canonical` off also
          makes a read return as soon as one byte has arrived.
    entry #command   the command to run
    entry #width   the terminal's width, in characters
    entry #height   the terminal's height, in characters
    entry #termios   the line discipline to set before the command starts, or `Unset` for the system's
    entry #exec   runs the command on its terminal to completion, reading its output as the type named
        As `Executable#exec`, but on a fresh pseudo-terminal: the output read is the
        terminal's, with standard error mixed in, and the exit status is reported as on pipes
        (`Exit.Fail(137)` for a killed command). Needs a `Computable` for the result, a
        `WorkingDirectory`, an `Environment`, a `Tactic[Exec.Error]` — raised for a command
        which cannot be found or started — and an `Exec.Event is Loggable`.
  entry #arguments   the program and its arguments, in order
  entry #detach   starts the command to outlive this program, keeping only its `Pid`
      The command's input is the null device, and its output and errors go nowhere, or are
      appended to the log file given (its parent directories made). See `Executable#detach`.
  entry #fork   starts the command and returns its `Job` without waiting
      Starts the program on pipes, in the `WorkingDirectory` and with the `Environment` in
      scope, logs `Exec.Event.ProcessStart`, and returns a `Job[Exec, result]^`. Raises
      `Exec.Error` if the program cannot be started. See `Executable#fork`.
  entry #pty   this command, to be run on a pseudo-terminal of the given size
      `pty(width = 80, height = 24, termios = Unset)` returns a `Command.OnTerminal`: nothing
      runs until its `exec` or a `session`. A `Termios` given here is set before the command
      starts; `Termios.raw` is what a full-screen program wants.
  entry #escape   the command as one line a POSIX shell parses back into these arguments
      Every argument single-quoted, with a single quote inside one written as `'\''`, since
      nothing is escapable inside single quotes. `Command(t"echo", t"a b").escape` is
      `'echo' 'a b'`. This is the form to hand a whole command to `sh -c`, `ssh` or anything
      else which re-parses it.

entry .Pipeline   several commands, each one's output the next's input, run as one
  detail
      Built with `|` (or `apply`) from `Command`s, and always flat: `#commands` holds every
      stage, however the pipeline was assembled. It is an `Executable`, run as a command is:
      `exec[T]()` reads the last stage's output as `T`, and the last stage's exit status is
      the pipeline's; `fork[T]()` gives a `Job` whose `stdin` writes to the first stage and
      whose streams read from the last (the JDK replaces every later stage's input with a
      stream which throws on write); `detach` starts every stage detached.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      (sh"echo 'a b c'" | sh"tr ' ' '\n'" | sh"wc -l").exec[Text]().trim   // t"3"
      ```

      On the JVM the stages are wired in the kernel by `ProcessBuilder.startPipeline`; on
      Scala Native each stage is started on pipes and a daemon thread copies its
      predecessor's output into its input, closing it at end of stream. A pipeline renders
      with `show` and `inspect` as its commands joined by ` | `.
  refer guillotine.Command
  refer guillotine.Executable
  entry .communicable   a pipeline as a message: its commands' `show` forms joined by ` | `
  entry .inspectable   renders a pipeline as its commands' `sh"…"` forms joined by ` | `
  entry .showable   renders a pipeline as its commands' shell forms joined by ` | `
  entry #commands   the stages, in order, flattened
  entry #detach   starts every stage detached, keeping only the last stage's `Pid`
      The first stage reads the null device, the last writes the log (or nowhere), and every
      stage's standard error goes where the last's output goes. See `Executable#detach`.
  entry #fork   starts every stage, wired together, and returns the `Job` of the whole
      Logs `Exec.Event.PipelineStart`. The job's output, exit status and `pid` are the last
      stage's; its `stdin` and `intake` write to the first. See `Executable#fork`.

entry .Job   a running subprocess, from `fork`: its streams, its result, its exit and its lifetime
  detail
      `Job[exec, result]` is what `Executable#fork[result]()` returns: a live handle to the
      process, tracked as a capability (fresh from `fork`, so it cannot be stored in an
      untracked value, though it may be shared among tasks: one draining each stream, another
      awaiting it, a third aborting it). It is a `Subprocess` (the streams and status a
      `Computable` reads), a `Process.Ref` (the pid and the signals), and `Durable`. Its
      `result` type was fixed at the fork, and `#await` reads it through the `Computable`
      in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val job = sh"cat".fork[Text]()
      job.alive                              // true
      job.stdin(Stream(Data(104, 105, 10)))  // writes, then closes the pipe
      job.await().trim                       // t"hi"
      job.exitStatus()                       // Exit.Ok
      ```

      For a pipeline the job has a process at each end: output, exit status and `pid` are the
      last stage's, and `stdin` and `intake` write to the first. A job on a pseudo-terminal is
      a `Command.OnTerminal.Job`, which adds `resize` and `mode`.
  refer guillotine.Executable
  refer guillotine.Computable
  refer guillotine.Process
  refer guillotine.Command.OnTerminal.Job
  entry .writable   `stream.writeTo(job)`: any stream the job's `Process.Input` can take, to its standard input
      The `Writable` given for a tracked `Job^`, by any chunk type for which `Process.Input
      is Writable` — `Data` (needing an `Emit[Truncation.Error]`) or `Text` (and a
      `Codepage`). It delegates to `#stdin`, which closes the pipe when the stream ends.
  entry .writableText   `text.writeTo(job)`: text encoded with the system codepage, to standard input
      The `Writable by Text` given for a tracked `Job^`, needing only an
      `Emit[Truncation.Error]`: the text is encoded with `Codepage.system`. This is what
      `t"hi\r".writeTo(terminal)` uses in a pseudo-terminal session.
  entry #pid   the process's `Pid`; a pipeline's is its last stage's
  entry #alive   whether the process is still running
  entry #attend   waits for the process to exit, discarding its status and output
  entry #stdout   standard output as a lazy byte stream, read on from the live pipe
      A `Stream[Data] over Credit`, pulled as it is forced; each call reads on from where the
      pipe is, and nothing is cached unless `memoize` is called. Needs a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, raised if the stream ends before its framing says it should.
  entry #stderr   standard error as a lazy byte stream
      As `#stdout`, from the error pipe. On a pseudo-terminal the error stream is empty, since
      the program's standard error is the terminal.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val job = sh"sh -c 'echo err 1>&2'".fork[Unit]()
      val bytes = job.stderr().memoize
      job.await()
      bytes.utf8.trim   // t"err"
      ```
  entry #text   the whole of standard output, as UTF-8 text, read to the end of the stream
      Blocks until the process closes its output (normally, until it exits).
  entry #errorText   the whole of standard error, as UTF-8 text, read to the end of the stream
  entry #lines   standard output as a lazy stream of its lines
      UTF-8, with `\n`, `\r\n` and `\r` each ending a line, as `BufferedReader.readLine`
      treats them; a `Stream[Array[Text]] over Credit` whose `records` are the lines one at a
      time. Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`. The `Chain[Text]` and `List[Text]`
      `Computable` instances read this.
  entry #status   waits for the process to exit, and gives its raw exit code
      `0` for success; for a process ended by a signal, 128 plus the signal number
      (`137` for `SIGKILL`). `#exitStatus` gives the same as an `Exit`, logged.
  entry #stdin   writes a whole stream to standard input and closes the pipe when it ends
      `job.stdin(stream)` takes a `Stream[chunk] over Credit` for any chunk type `Process.Input
      is Writable by` — `Data`, or `Text` with a `Codepage` in scope — writes it, and closes
      the process's input, which suits a program that reads its input to the end and exits.
      For a pipeline the stream goes to the first stage. A long-lived child driven message by
      message wants `#intake` instead.
  entry #intake   standard input as a push endpoint, for driving a live process incrementally
      An `Intake[Data] over Credit` on the process's input pipe: each `put` is written and
      `flush` sends it, with the pipe left open, so a language server or a REPL can be fed a
      message, its reply read, and another sent; `finish` closes the pipe. Needs a
      `java.io.OutputStream is Sink by Data over Credit`, which turbulence provides.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val job = sh"cat".fork[Text]()
      val intake = job.intake
      intake.put(Data(104, 105, 10))
      intake.flush()
      job.alive              // true: the pipe is open and cat is still echoing
      intake.put(Data(98, 121, 101, 10))
      intake.finish()
      job.await().trim       // t"hi\nbye"
      ```
  entry #await   the job's result, through the `Computable` in scope, now or within a duration
      `await()` computes the `result` the job was forked for: for `Text` it waits for the
      output to end, for `Exit` for the process to exit, for `Chain[Text]` it returns at once
      with a lazy chain. It needs the `result is Computable` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, raised if the output ends before its framing says it should.

      `await(duration)` takes any duration type `Abstractable across Durations to Long`
      (nanoseconds; a plain `Long` of milliseconds with
      `abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable`), waits at most that long for the process to
      exit, and then computes the result; if it has not exited, raises
      `Async.Error(Async.Error.Reason.Timeout)` through a `Tactic[Async.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val job = sh"sleep 1".fork[Unit]()
      val outcome = capture[Async.Error](job.await(50L))   // Reason.Timeout
      job.kill()
      ```
  entry #exitStatus   waits for the process to exit and gives its status as an `Exit`, logged
      `Exit.Ok` for zero, otherwise `Exit.Fail(code)`; logs `Exec.Event.ProcessExit` at `fine`
      for zero and as a warning otherwise, so it needs an `Exec.Event is Loggable`. A killed
      process reports `Exit.Fail(137)`, and one interrupted by `^C` at its terminal
      `Exit.Fail(130)`.
  entry #abort   asks the process to stop, with `SIGTERM`
      `destroy()`: the process may catch the signal and clean up, or ignore it. Logs
      `Exec.Event.AbortProcess` at `info`.
  entry #kill   stops the process, with `SIGKILL`
      `destroyForcibly()`: the process cannot ignore it. Logs `Exec.Event.KillProcess` as a
      warning.
  entry #process   the operating-system `Process` behind the job, by its pid
      `Process(pid)`, so it needs a `Tactic[Pid.Error]`, raised if the process has already
      gone; the result is a tracked `Process^`, with the parent, children and process tree
      a `Job` does not have.
  entry #startTime   when the process started, as any instant type, or `Unset`
      Through `Process#startTime`, swallowing a `Pid.Error` as `Unset`; takes an instant type
      `Instantiable across Instants from Long` (milliseconds). Always `Unset` on Scala Native.
  entry #cpuUsage   the CPU time the process has used, as any duration type, or `Unset`
      Through `Process#cpuUsage`; takes a duration type `Instantiable across Durations from
      Long` (nanoseconds). Always `Unset` on Scala Native.

entry .Subprocess   what a `Computable` reads: a running process's streams and exit status, platform-free
  detail
      The trait a `Computable` instance computes its result from: standard output and error
      as lazy byte streams or as whole text, the output's lines, and the exit status, with no
      mention of `java.lang.Process` or any other platform type. `Job` is its one
      implementation. A new `Computable` is written against this.
  refer guillotine.Computable
  refer guillotine.Job
  entry #stdout   standard output as a lazy byte stream; needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`
  entry #stderr   standard error as a lazy byte stream; needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`
  entry #text   the whole of standard output as UTF-8 text, read to its end
  entry #errorText   the whole of standard error as UTF-8 text, read to its end
  entry #lines   standard output as a lazy stream of lines; needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`
  entry #status   waits for the exit, and gives the raw exit code

entry .Computable   how a process's output becomes a result of type `T`: the typeclass behind `exec[T]`
  detail
      `T is Computable` says how to read a `Subprocess` as a `T`: which stream, when, and what
      the exit status means. `exec[T]()` and `Job#await()` summon it for their type
      parameter, so the type named *is* the interpretation. The instances in the companion,
      found with no import:

      - `Text`, `String`: the whole of standard output, when the process closes it.
      - `List[Text]`: the lines of standard output, when it ends.
      - `Chain[Text]`: the lines as a lazy, memoizing chain, pulled as the process writes
        them, so the call returns at once; needs a `Buffering`. (An exclusive `Iterator`
        cannot be the result of the erased `Intelligible` evidence, which is why the
        line-at-a-time type is a `Chain`.)
      - `Data`: the whole of standard output as bytes, memoized; needs a `Buffering`. For the
        bytes lazily, use `Subprocess#stdout()` on a forked `Job`.
      - `Exit`: the exit status, `Exit.Ok` or `Exit.Fail(code)`; non-zero is an answer, not an
        error.
      - `Unit`: waits for the exit and discards everything.
      - `Stderr`: standard error, as `Stderr(text)` (the given is in `Stderr`'s companion).
      - any path type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`: standard output, trimmed, decoded
        as the path — `sh"which git".exec[Path on Linux]()`.

      A type not covered gains an instance by mapping one (`Computable` is a functor) or
      through the `.apply` factory, taking the `Subprocess` and the `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`
      its reads need:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Count(value: Int)

      given Count is Computable = summon[Text is Computable].map: text =>
        Count(text.trim.s.toInt)

      given List[Data] is Computable = Computable: (process, tactic) =>
        given Tactic[Truncation.Error] = tactic
        process.stdout().to[List]
      ```

      A read cut short — the stream ending before its framing says it should — is a
      `Truncation.Error` through the tactic, which `exec` reports as `Exec.Error` for the
      command.
  refer guillotine.Subprocess
  refer guillotine.Executable
  refer guillotine.Stderr
  entry .apply   makes an instance from a function of the `Subprocess` and the truncation tactic
      `Computable[T]((process, tactic) => …)`: the factory to write an instance with, since a
      method with a `using` clause is not a single abstract method and a lambda does not
      convert to one. The tactic is passed as a plain second argument; bind it with
      `given Tactic[Truncation.Error] = tactic` where a read needs it.
  entry .lineStream   `Chain[Text]`: the lines of standard output, lazily, as the process writes them
      Needs a `Buffering` in scope. The chain is pure and memoizing: forcing it pulls the next
      line from the live pipe, and a line once read is kept.
  entry .list   `List[Text]`: the lines of standard output, once it has ended
  entry .text   `Text`: the whole of standard output, UTF-8, once the process closes it
  entry .string   `String`: as `.text`, as a Java string
  entry .data   `Data`: the whole of standard output as bytes, memoized; needs a `Buffering`
  entry .exitStatus   `Exit`: `Exit.Ok` for zero, `Exit.Fail(code)` otherwise, once the process exits
  entry .unit   `Unit`: waits for the process to exit and discards its output and status
  entry .instantiable   any path type: standard output, trimmed, decoded as the path
      For a type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`, such as `Path on Linux`: what
      `sh"which git".exec[Path on Linux]()` and the `which` command's implied result use.
  entry #compute   reads the `Subprocess` as a `Self`, raising `Truncation.Error` for a read cut short
  entry #map   an instance for another type, by a function of this one's result
      `summon[Text is Computable].map(_.trim)` is a `Text is Computable` which trims, and
      `map(Count(_))` one for a `Count`: the usual way to cover a new type.

entry .Stderr   a process's standard error as text: `exec[Stderr]()`
  detail
      A wrapper for a result read from the error stream rather than the output stream. Its
      companion's `Computable` instance reads the whole of standard error, once the process
      closes it, as UTF-8 text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      sh"sh -c 'echo oops 1>&2'".exec[Stderr]().text.trim   // t"oops"
      ```
  refer guillotine.Computable
  entry .computable   reads the whole of standard error, as text, into a `Stderr`
  entry #text   what the process wrote to standard error

entry .Process   a handle to any process on the system, by `Pid`: its tree, lifetime and signals
  detail
      A `Process` wraps a `ProcessHandle`: a live, tracked handle to an operating-system
      process, whether this program started it or not. `Process()` is the current process,
      `Process(pid)` any running one (raising `Pid.Error` if none has that pid), `Process.all`
      every process visible to this one and `Process.roots` those with no parent. It is a
      `Process.Ref` — `pid`, `alive`, `attend()`, `kill()`, `abort()` — with `#parent` and
      `#children` for walking the tree, and `Durable`. It is a shared capability: several
      tasks may observe it and one may abort it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val me: Process = Process()
      me.pid
      me.parent.lay(t"none")(_.pid.show)
      me.children.map(_.pid)
      ```

      Under `import soundness.*`, `Process` is this type, not `java.lang.Process`; write the
      latter in full where it is meant. A `Job` reaches its own `Process` with `Job#process`.
  refer guillotine.Pid
  refer guillotine.Job
  entry .apply   `Process()`: this process; `Process(pid)`: the running process with that pid
      `Process(pid)` needs a `Tactic[Pid.Error]`, raised when no process with that pid is
      running. The result is tracked (`Process^`), so it may be used but not stored in an
      untracked value; take its `pid` or another plain value out of a `capture` or a task.
  entry .all   every process visible to this one
  entry .roots   every visible process which has no parent
  entry .Input   a process's standard input as a sink, written to by `Job#stdin`
    detail
        The type a `Job`'s input is written through, so that `java.io.OutputStream` never
        appears in the API: `Process.Input is Writable by Data` and `by Text` are the givens
        in its companion, and `Job.writable` lifts them to the job itself. A user does not
        construct one; its constructor is internal.
    entry .data   writes a `Data` stream to a process's input; needs an `Emit[Truncation.Error]`
    entry .text   writes a `Text` stream to a process's input, through the `Codepage` in scope
        Needs a `Codepage` (`codepages.utf8Codepage`) and an `Emit[Truncation.Error]`.
  entry .Ref   what every process handle can do: `pid`, `alive`, `attend`, `kill`, `abort`, timings
    detail
        The trait `Process` and `Job` share: a reference to a process on the system, by pid,
        with its lifetime, the two signals, and its start time and CPU usage where the platform
        reports them. Code which only needs to wait for or stop a process can take a
        `Process.Ref` and be handed either.
    entry #pid   the process's `Pid`
    entry #kill   stops the process with `SIGKILL`; needs an `Exec.Event is Loggable`
    entry #abort   asks the process to stop with `SIGTERM`; needs an `Exec.Event is Loggable`
    entry #alive   whether the process is still running
    entry #attend   waits for the process to exit
    entry #startTime   when the process started, as any instant type `Instantiable across Instants from Long`, or `Unset`
    entry #cpuUsage   the CPU time used, as any duration type `Instantiable across Durations from Long`, or `Unset`
  entry #pid   the process's `Pid`
  entry #kill   stops the process with `SIGKILL`, through `ProcessHandle.destroyForcibly`
  entry #abort   asks the process to stop with `SIGTERM`, through `ProcessHandle.destroy`
  entry #alive   whether the process is still running
  entry #attend   blocks until the process exits
  entry #parent   the parent process, or `Unset` for a process with none
      `Process().parent` is the shell or launcher which started this program. The element is
      a tracked `Process`, so match on it or take its `pid` with `lay`.
  entry #children   the process's direct children, as `Process` handles
  entry #startTime   when the process started, in any instant type, from `ProcessHandle.Info`
      Takes a type `Instantiable across Instants from Long` (milliseconds since the epoch):
      `startTime[Moment]` with aviation. `Unset` when the platform does not report it, and
      always on Scala Native, whose javalib lacks `java.time`.
  entry #cpuUsage   the total CPU time the process has used, in any duration type
      Takes a type `Instantiable across Durations from Long` (nanoseconds). `Unset` when the
      platform does not report it, and always on Scala Native.

entry .Pid   a process identifier, as its own type: renders as `↯1234`, parses from a number
  detail
      `Pid(value: Long)` is a process id, distinct from an exit code or a descriptor number
      so the three cannot be confused. It shows, encodes and inspects as the number marked
      with `↯` (`Pid(1234L).show` is `↯1234`), decodes from a plain number (`t"42".as[Pid]`),
      and substitutes into `sh"…"` as its shown form. `Job#pid` and `Process#pid` give one,
      `Process(pid)` looks one up, and `Executable#detach` returns one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Pid(123L).show      // t"↯123"
      t"42".as[Pid]       // Pid(42L)
      t"abc".as[Pid]      // raises Number.Error
      ```
  refer guillotine.Process
  refer guillotine.Pid.Error
  entry .showable   renders a pid as `↯` followed by its number
  entry .encodable   encodes a pid in text, as `↯1234`
  entry .inspectable   the debug rendering, the same `↯1234`
  entry .decodable   reads a pid from a decimal number in text, raising `Number.Error` otherwise
      `t"42".as[Pid]` is `Pid(42L)`; the text is parsed as a `Long`, and a malformed value
      raises `Number.Error(text, Long, Unparseable)` through a `Tactic[Number.Error]`.
  entry .sshAgentPid   the `SSH_AGENT_PID` environment variable, read as a `Pid`
      An ambience `Variable["sshAgentPid", Pid]`, so `Environment.sshAgentPid` (with an
      `Environment` in scope) is the pid of the running SSH agent; raises `Number.Error` if
      the variable is not a number. Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`.
  entry .Error   raised when no process with a given pid is running
    detail
        Error 909: _the process with PID `1234` is not running_. Raised by `Process(pid)`, and
        so by `Job#process`, when `ProcessHandle.of` finds nothing: the process has exited,
        or never existed. Needs a `Diagnostics` in scope to construct.
    entry #pid   the pid that was looked up
  entry #value   the number

entry .Exec   the vocabulary of running a command: `Exec.Error` and the `Exec.Event`s logged
  detail
      A namespace, not a type: an execution itself is a `Job` or a `Process`. `Exec.Error`
      is raised when a command cannot be run, and `Exec.Event` is what the starts, exits and
      signals log. (`Executable#Exec`, the type member holding a command's name, is a
      different thing which shares the word.)
  refer guillotine.Executable
  entry .Error   raised when a command cannot be run: a missing binary, or a read of its output cut short
    detail
        Error 691: _execution of the command `ls -la` failed_. Raised by `exec`, `fork`,
        `detach` and `Command.OnTerminal#exec` when the program cannot be started — it is not
        on the `PATH`, or the file is not executable — and by `exec` when a read of the output
        is cut short (a `Truncation.Error`, re-raised as this for the command; the last
        command of a pipeline). It names the `#command`, so a failure reports what was
        attempted rather than an errno; the `#stdout` and `#stderr` fields are empty unless
        the raiser had output to attach.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import logging.silentLogging
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

        capture[Exec.Error](sh"no-such-binary".exec[Text]()).command.arguments.head
        // t"no-such-binary"
        ```

        A program which starts and exits non-zero does not raise this: its status is its
        result, read as an `Exit`.
    entry #command   the command which could not be run
    entry #stdout   any standard output attached, as `Data`; empty by default
    entry #stderr   any standard error attached, as `Data`; empty by default
  entry .Event   what running a command logs: starts, exits, aborts and kills
    detail
        Every `Event` is tagged `Log.Process`. `ProcessStart(command)` is logged at `info` by
        `fork`, `exec`, `detach` and a terminal session; `PipelineStart(commands)` likewise for
        a pipeline; `ProcessExit(pid, code)` by `Job#exitStatus()`, at `fine` for zero and as
        a warning otherwise; `AbortProcess(pid)` at `info` by `Job#abort()`; and
        `KillProcess(pid)` as a warning by `Job#kill()`. An `Exec.Event is Loggable` must be
        in scope wherever these run; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
        _starting the process `ls -la`_, _starting the pipeline `cat x` `wc -l`_, _the process
        with PID `↯1234` exited with code `0`_, _aborted the process with PID `↯1234`_,
        _killed the process with PID `↯1234`_.

entry .Intelligible   evidence that a command's name implies its result type, for `sh"…"()`
  detail
      `"ls" is Intelligible to Chain[Text]` says that a command named `ls` is read as a
      `Chain[Text]` when it is applied with no type: `sh"ls -la"()` summons the `Intelligible`
      evidence for the `Exec` type member of the command (its name, as a literal type) and
      runs `exec` with the evidence's `Result` type (inherited from `Resultant`). The trait is sealed and its instances are
      erased: they exist only for the compiler to choose a type, and cost nothing at run
      time. The companion extends `PosixCommands`, so every POSIX utility's evidence is in
      implicit scope with no import; a command outside that list has no implied type and is
      run with `exec[T]()`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      sh"echo hi"()          // Text
      sh"ls"()               // Chain[Text]
      sh"mkdir -p tmp"()     // Exit
      ```

      An instance for a program of one's own is an `erased given ("mytool" is Intelligible
      to Text)` — written as the `PosixCommands` givens are — but its construction needs the
      companion's private `Evidence` class, so in practice the list is the library's.
  refer guillotine.PosixCommands
  refer guillotine.Executable
  refer guillotine.Computable
  entry #Self   the command's name, as a literal type such as `"ls"`

entry .PosixCommands   the result type each POSIX utility implies, one erased given per command
  detail
      The trait holding an `Intelligible` given for each of the POSIX shell utilities, mixed
      into `Intelligible`'s companion so they are found with no import. Each says what
      `sh"<command> …"()` returns: `Text` for a utility whose output is a report read whole
      (`echo`, `date`, `wc`, `pwd`, `uname`, most of the list); `Chain[Text]` for one whose
      output is lines worth reading as they arrive (`cat`, `grep`, `head`, `ls`, `man`,
      `sed`, `sort`, `strings`, `tail`, `uniq`); `Exit` for one whose effect, not its output,
      is the point (`chgrp`, `chmod`, `chown`, `cp`, `kill`, `ln`, `mkdir`, `mkfifo`,
      `mknod`, `mv`, `newgrp`, `rm`, `rmdir`, `sleep`, `touch`); and for `which`, any path
      type the caller asks for. All are `erased`: no instance exists at run time, and the
      choice is made entirely by the compiler. The given for `wait` is named `waitCommand`,
      since `wait` is a method of every object.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val lines: Chain[Text] = sh"ls -la"()
      val today: Text = sh"date"()
      val made: Exit = sh"mkdir -p build"()
      ```
  refer guillotine.Intelligible
  refer guillotine.Computable
  entry #alias   `alias` (lists or defines shell aliases) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #ar   `ar` (maintains archive libraries) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #at   `at` (queues a command to run later) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #awk   `awk` (the pattern-scanning language) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #basename   `basename` (strips a path to its last segment) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #batch   `batch` (queues a command to run when the load allows) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #bc   `bc` (the arbitrary-precision calculator) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #cat   `cat` (concatenates files) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #chgrp   `chgrp` (changes a file's group) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #chmod   `chmod` (changes a file's mode) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #chown   `chown` (changes a file's owner) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #cksum   `cksum` (a file's CRC checksum and size) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #cmp   `cmp` (compares two files byte by byte) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #comm   `comm` (the lines two sorted files share or do not) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #command   `command` (runs a command bypassing shell functions) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #cp   `cp` (copies files) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #crontab   `crontab` (edits the cron table) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #csplit   `csplit` (splits a file by context) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #cut   `cut` (selects columns of each line) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #date   `date` (prints or sets the date) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #dd   `dd` (copies and converts blocks) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #df   `df` (free disk space) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #diff   `diff` (the differences between two files) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #dirname   `dirname` (strips a path to its directory) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #du   `du` (disk usage) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #echo   `echo` (writes its arguments) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #ed   `ed` (the line editor) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #env   `env` (prints or modifies the environment) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #expand   `expand` (turns tabs into spaces) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #expr   `expr` (evaluates an expression) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #file   `file` (guesses a file's type) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #find   `find` (walks a directory tree) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #fold   `fold` (wraps long lines) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #gencat   `gencat` (builds a message catalogue) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #getconf   `getconf` (configuration values) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #getopts   `getopts` (parses option arguments) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #grep   `grep` (the lines matching a pattern) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #hash   `hash` (the shell's command hash table) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #head   `head` (the first lines of a file) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #iconv   `iconv` (converts between character encodings) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #id   `id` (the user's and groups' identities) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #join   `join` (joins two files on a common field) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #kill   `kill` (sends a signal) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #ln   `ln` (makes a link) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #locale   `locale` (locale information) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #localedef   `localedef` (compiles a locale definition) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #logger   `logger` (writes to the system log) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #logname   `logname` (the login name) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #lp   `lp` (sends a file to a printer) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #ls   `ls` (lists a directory) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #m4   `m4` (the macro processor) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #mailx   `mailx` (sends and reads mail) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #man   `man` (a manual page) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #mesg   `mesg` (permits or denies messages to the terminal) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #mkdir   `mkdir` (makes a directory) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #mkfifo   `mkfifo` (makes a named pipe) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #mknod   `mknod` (makes a special file) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #mv   `mv` (moves files) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #newgrp   `newgrp` (changes the current group) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #od   `od` (dumps a file in octal or another base) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #paste   `paste` (joins files line by line) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #patch   `patch` (applies a diff) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #patchchk   `patchchk` (checks a patch) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #pax   `pax` (the portable archiver) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #pr   `pr` (paginates a file for printing) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #printf   `printf` (formatted output) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #ps   `ps` (the running processes) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #pwd   `pwd` (the working directory) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #read   `read` (reads a line into variables) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #renice   `renice` (alters a process's priority) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #rm   `rm` (removes files) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #rmdir   `rmdir` (removes an empty directory) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #sed   `sed` (the stream editor) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #sleep   `sleep` (pauses for a time) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #sort   `sort` (sorts lines) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #split   `split` (splits a file into pieces) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #strings   `strings` (the printable strings in a binary) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #stty   `stty` (reads or sets the terminal's line discipline) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #tabs   `tabs` (sets the terminal's tab stops) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #tail   `tail` (the last lines of a file) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #tee   `tee` (copies input to files and to output) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #test   `test` (evaluates a condition) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #touch   `touch` (updates a file's timestamps, making it if needed) runs as `Exit`: its status is its result
  entry #tput   `tput` (terminal capabilities) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #tr   `tr` (translates characters) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #tsort   `tsort` (a topological sort) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #tty   `tty` (the terminal's device name) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #umask   `umask` (the file creation mask) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #unalias   `unalias` (removes an alias) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #uname   `uname` (the system's name) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #unexpand   `unexpand` (turns spaces into tabs) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #uniq   `uniq` (drops or counts repeated lines) runs as `Chain[Text]`: its lines, lazily
  entry #uudecode   `uudecode` (decodes a uuencoded file) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #uuencode   `uuencode` (encodes a file for mail) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
  entry #waitCommand   `wait` (waits for a background job) runs as `Text`: the whole of its output, once it ends
