  entry #which   `which` (finds a program on the `PATH`) runs as the path type the caller asks for
      An inline given, for any type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`: `val git: Path on
      Linux = sh"which git"()` reads the trimmed output as a path, through the `Computable`
      instance for path types. The type must be fixed by the expected type, since the
      command alone cannot choose it.

entry .Parameterizable   how a value of a type is rendered as a command argument in `sh"…"`
  detail
      `T is Parameterizable` lets a `T` be substituted into the `sh"…"` interpolator:
      `#show` renders it as the text of one argument, which the interpolator then escapes for
      its position, so the instance never concerns itself with quoting. The companion's
      `.encodable` covers every type `Encodable in Text` — a `Path`, a URL, a `Pid` — and a
      type with neither is a compile error at the substitution. `Text`, `List[Text]` and
      `Command` substitute through their own `Sh` insertions rather than this.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Tag(value: Int)
      given Tag is Parameterizable = summon[Text is Parameterizable].contramap(tag => t"<${tag.value}>")

      sh"echo ${Tag(7)}"   // Command(t"echo", t"<7>")
      ```
  refer guillotine.sh
  entry .encodable   any type `Encodable in Text` substitutes as its encoding
      A `Path on Linux` substitutes as `/etc/hosts`, a `Pid` as `↯42`, a URL as its text.
  entry #show   the value as the text of one command argument
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by a function to this one's
      `summon[Text is Parameterizable].contramap(f)` is the usual way to write an instance for
      a type which has a textual form of its own.

entry .Termios   a terminal's line discipline: the `stty` switches a program most often changes
  detail
      The settings of a pseudo-terminal which decide how what is typed reaches the program
      and how what it writes reaches the screen, named after the POSIX `termios` flags they
      stand for, each a `Boolean` defaulting to on. `.cooked` is everything on — line editing,
      echo, signals and the translations a shell expects — and `.raw` everything off, as a
      full-screen program sets before it draws. A `Termios` given to `Command#pty` is set
      before the command starts, and a running command's is read and changed through
      `Command.OnTerminal.Job#mode`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Termios.raw.show                               // t"-echo -icanon -isig -iexten -icrnl -ixon -opost"
      Termios(echo = false)                          // cooked, but unechoed: for typing a password
      Termios.raw == Termios(false, false, false, false, false, false, false)
      ```
  refer guillotine.Command.OnTerminal
  entry .cooked   every switch on: input edited and echoed a line at a time, `^C` a signal
  entry .raw   every switch off: each byte delivered as typed, unechoed, `^C` an ordinary byte
      What `stty raw -echo` sets, and what a full-screen program such as an editor needs.
      Turning `canonical` off also sets the read to return each byte as it arrives.
  entry .showable   renders the switches as `stty` reports them: `echo -icanon isig …`
      Each switch by its POSIX flag name, prefixed with `-` when off, in the order `echo`,
      `icanon`, `isig`, `iexten`, `icrnl`, `ixon`, `opost`.
  entry #echo   `echo`: whether typed characters are echoed back to the terminal
  entry #canonical   `icanon`: whether input is gathered and edited a line at a time, or delivered as typed
  entry #signals   `isig`: whether `^C` and `^Z` signal the foreground program
  entry #extensions   `iexten`: whether the extra line-editing characters are interpreted
  entry #translateReturn   `icrnl`: whether a typed carriage return arrives as a newline
  entry #flowControl   `ixon`: whether `^S` and `^Q` pause and resume output
  entry #postprocessing   `opost` with `onlcr`: whether a newline the program writes is sent as carriage return and newline

entry .Sh   the `sh"…"` interpolator's parser and its insertion rules
  detail
      The machinery behind `sh"…"`: the parser (`.Runtime`) which both the macro runs at
      compile time, to check the literal, and the generated code runs at run time, to
      assemble the arguments with the substituted values; the parser's `.State` and
      `.Context`; the `.Parameters` a substitution contributes; the `.Error` a malformed
      literal raises; and the contextual `Insertion` givens which say how a `Text`, a
      `List[Text]`, a `Command` or any `Parameterizable` value becomes parameters. A program
      does not call these directly, but a `given Insertion[Sh.Parameters, T]` in scope at the
      interpolation is one way to substitute a type, where `Parameterizable` is the usual.
  refer guillotine.sh
  refer guillotine.Parameterizable
  entry .Context   where the parser is: `Awaiting` a word, in an `Unquoted` word, in `Quotes2` or `Quotes1`
      `Awaiting` is between arguments, `Unquoted` inside a bare word, `Quotes2` inside double
      quotes and `Quotes1` inside single quotes, where a backslash is literal.
  entry .State   the parser's state: context, a pending escape, the arguments so far, and offsets
    detail
        `State(current, escape, arguments, offset, quoteStart)`: the `Context`, whether the
        last character was an unconsumed backslash, the arguments completed and in progress,
        how many characters have been consumed (each substitution counting as one), and the
        offset of the opening quote of the current quoted context, or `-1`, so that an
        unclosed quote is reported where it was opened.
    entry #current   the parsing context
    entry #escape   whether a backslash is pending
    entry #arguments   the arguments so far, the last possibly still being built
    entry #offset   characters consumed, each substitution counting as one
    entry #quoteStart   the offset of the open quote of the current quoted context, or `-1`
  entry .Parameters   what one substitution contributes: zero or more argument texts
    detail
        A `Text` contributes one, a `List[Text]` one per element, a `Command` its arguments.
        Unquoted, each is an argument of its own (joined to any adjacent literal text);
        inside quotes they are joined with spaces into the one argument the quotes delimit.
    entry #params   the texts contributed
  entry .Error   a malformed `sh"…"` literal: an unclosed quote or a stray escape, with its offset
    detail
        Thrown by the parser and reported by the macro as a compile error at the offending
        character: _this double quote is never closed_, _this single quote is never closed_,
        _a command cannot end with an escape character_, _an escape character cannot appear
        immediately before a substitution_. At run time, the literal having already been
        checked, it cannot arise.
    entry #detail   the message
    entry #offset   where in the literal the fault is, counting each substitution as one character
  entry .Runtime   the parser: `initial`, `parse`, `insert`, `skip` and `complete`
    detail
        A small state machine over the characters of the literal parts, which the macro drives
        with `skip` in place of each substitution to check the literal, and the generated code
        drives with `insert` to splice the real values. Not for direct use.
    entry .initial   the state before any character: awaiting a word, with no arguments
    entry .parse   feeds the characters of a literal part to the state
    entry .insert   splices a substitution's `Parameters` into the state, according to its context
    entry .skip   advances past a substitution at compile time, when its value is not yet known
    entry .complete   finishes: the `Command`, or `Sh.Error` for an unclosed quote or trailing escape
  entry .nothing   a `Nothing` substitution contributes the empty argument
  entry .text   a `Text` substitution is exactly one argument, whatever it contains
  entry .list   a `List[Text]` substitution is one argument per element, or, in quotes, one joined by spaces
  entry .command   a `Command` substitution is its arguments, so `sh"sh -c '$inner'"` passes it as one
  entry .parameterizable   any `Parameterizable` value substitutes as its `show` text, as one argument

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind `sh"…"`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the `sh` macro has a stable home.
  entry .sh   the macro: parses the literal parts at compile time, positions errors, and splices the runtime parse
      Checks the literal with `Sh.Runtime` (reporting a `Sh.Error` at the character which
      caused it, mapped back through `$$` and unicode escapes to the source), summons an
      `Insertion[Sh.Parameters, T]` for each substituted type (failing with _can't substitute
      `T` into a sh-interpolator_), and produces a `Command` refined with its first word as
      the `Exec` type.
